Ahh good thinking 💡 — you want a mini dictionary of important JavaScript string & array methods (like slice, join) that often come in interviews and coding exercises.
I’ll give you a practical list with short explanations + examples.

🔑 Important String Methods
Method	What it Does	Example
charAt(i)	Returns character at index	"apple".charAt(0) → "a"
toUpperCase() / toLowerCase()	Changes case	"apple".toUpperCase() → "APPLE"
slice(start, end?)	Extracts part of string	"banana".slice(1, 4) → "ana"
substring(start, end?)	Similar to slice, but doesn’t allow negative indexes	"banana".substring(1, 4) → "ana"
split(separator)	Splits string into array	"a,b,c".split(",") → ["a","b","c"]
includes(sub)	Checks if substring exists	"hello".includes("he") → true
replace(old, new)	Replace part of string	"hi hi".replace("hi","bye") → "bye hi"
trim()	Removes spaces from start & end	" hello ".trim() → "hello"
🔑 Important Array Methods
Method	What it Does	Example
push()	Add element at end	[1,2].push(3) → [1,2,3] -- Return value is new length
pop()	Remove element from end	[1,2,3].pop() → [1,2] -- return is the last removed element
unshift()	Add element at start	[2,3].unshift(1) → [1,2,3]
shift()	Remove element from start	[1,2,3].shift() → [2,3]
join(sep)	Joins array into string	["a","b"].join("-") → "a-b"
concat()	Combines arrays	[1,2].concat([3,4]) → [1,2,3,4]
@@= slice(start,end)	Extracts part of array	[1,2,3,4].slice(1,3) → [2,3]
@@= splice(start, count, ...items)	Add/remove at specific index	arr.splice(1,1,"x")
@@= map()	Transform each element → new array	[1,2].map(x=>x*2) → [2,4]
@@= filter()	Keep only matching elements	[1,2,3].filter(x=>x>1) → [2,3]
@@= reduce()	Reduce array to single value	[1,2,3].reduce((a,b)=>a+b,0) → 6
find()	Find first matching element	[1,2,3].find(x=>x>1) → 2
findIndex()	Find index of element	[1,2,3].findIndex(x=>x>1) → 1
@@= sort()	Sort elements (default = string sort)	[10,2,5].sort() → [10,2,5] (⚠️ need custom compare)
reverse()	Reverse array order	[1,2,3].reverse() → [3,2,1]
includes()	Checks if value exists	[1,2,3].includes(2) → true

✅ With just these methods, you can solve 90% of JS coding interview tasks.

============================================================================================================

🔹 Methods that CHANGE the existing (mutating)

push() → adds elements to end of array

pop() → removes last element

shift() → removes first element

unshift() → adds elements to beginning

splice() → add/remove/replace elements

sort() → sorts array in place

🔹 Methods that RETURN a NEW (non-mutating)

slice() → extracts portion (new array/string)

substring() → extracts portion (new string)

split() → splits string into array

replace() → returns new string with replaced value

trim() → returns new string without spaces

includes() → returns boolean (true/false)

join() → returns string from array

map() → returns new array

filter() → returns new array

reduce() → returns a single value

charAt() → returns character at index

toUpperCase() / toLowerCase() → returns new string

⚡ Quick memory hack:

If it changes length/order/content of array → mutating. (Changes the existing array)

If it just reads or gives copy → non-mutating. (returns new array)




